NNAAMMEE
     ttrruussss — trace system calls

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ttrruussss [--ffaacceeddDDHHSS] [--oo _f_i_l_e] [--ss _s_t_r_s_i_z_e] --pp _p_i_d
     ttrruussss [--ffaacceeddDDHHSS] [--oo _f_i_l_e] [--ss _s_t_r_s_i_z_e] _c_o_m_m_a_n_d [_a_r_g_s]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ttrruussss utility traces the system calls called by the specified process
     or program.  Output is to the specified output file, or standard error by
     default.  It does this by stopping and restarting the process being moni‐
     tored via ptrace(2).

     The options are as follows:

     --ff      Trace descendants of the original traced process created by
             fork(2), vfork(2), etc.  To distinguish events between processes,
             the process ID (PID) of the process is included in the output of
             each event.

     --aa      Show the argument strings that are passed in each execve(2) sys‐
             tem call.

     --cc      Do not display individual system calls or signals.  Instead, be‐
             fore exiting, print a summary containing for each system call:
             the total system time used, the number of times the call was in‐
             voked, and the number of times the call returned with an error.

     --ee      Show the environment strings that are passed in each execve(2)
             system call.

     --dd      Include timestamps in the output showing the time elapsed since
             the trace was started.

     --DD      Include timestamps in the output showing the time elapsed since
             the last recorded event.

     --HH      Include the thread ID of in the output of each event.

     --SS      Do not display information about signals received by the process.
             (Normally, ttrruussss displays signal as well as system call events.)

     --oo _f_i_l_e
             Print the output to the specified _f_i_l_e instead of standard error.

     --ss _s_t_r_s_i_z_e
             Display strings using at most _s_t_r_s_i_z_e characters.  If the buffer
             is larger, “...” will be displayed at the end of the string.  The
             default _s_t_r_s_i_z_e is 32.

     --pp _p_i_d  Follow the process specified by _p_i_d instead of a new command.

     _c_o_m_m_a_n_d [_a_r_g_s]
             Execute _c_o_m_m_a_n_d and trace the system calls of it.  (The --pp and
             _c_o_m_m_a_n_d options are mutually exclusive.)

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     Follow the system calls used in echoing "hello":
           $ truss /bin/echo hello

     Do the same, but put the output into a file:
           $ truss -o /tmp/truss.out /bin/echo hello

     Follow an already-running process:
           $ truss -p 34

